> The implemented variant and its consistency equations

Stentz's original D* and Koenig–Likhachev's D* Lite are related
incremental-search algorithms with different internal machinery. This
report implements D* Lite. The earlier d_star_replan body ran a cold
A* search on every call; it had no retained labels or repair queue.
The new one-shot wrapper preserves the original interface, and the
persistent class makes reuse explicit rather than implying that a
sequence of independent calls shares state.

For N grid cells, every shortest reachable route can be chosen simple
and has at most N−1 unit edges. Exact uint64_t labels use a named
infinity sentinel. Backups at N or larger are saturated to infinity,
which cannot remove a possible shortest route. Dimensions and total
cell count are validated, with a mandatory INT_MAX cell bound so
legacy double route costs remain exact integers.

For a free goal, rhs(goal)=0. A blocked goal has an infinite boundary;
unblocking it restores zero. Every blocked non-goal cell has infinite
rhs. At any other free cell,

\[
\operatorname{rhs}(u)=\min_{v\in\text{free four-neighbors}(u)}\{1+g(v)\}.
\]

A cell is locally consistent when g(u)=rhs(u). The indexed binary heap
contains exactly the inconsistent cells, at most one entry per cell.
Let m(u)=min(g(u),rhs(u)) and h be Manhattan distance. Its mathematical
priority is the lexicographic pair

\[
K(u)=\bigl(m(u)+h(start,u)+k_m,\ m(u)\bigr).
\]

Remaining heap ties use row-major cell id. A free move costs one, so
Manhattan distance is an admissible and consistent lower bound even
when obstacles force a detour. The next queued vertex is processed as
follows: refresh a stale moving-start key; lower g to rhs when g>rhs;
or raise g to infinity when g<rhs, then recompute affected backups.
Each changed occupancy cell updates itself and its in-bounds neighbors,
covering all edges whose cost changed.

Repair stops when the start is locally consistent and no smaller queued
key remains. D* Lite's consistency/priority argument then gives the
start's optimal cost. It does not require every other cell to be solved;
unused frontier labels can remain stale or infinite. Route extraction
selects a free successor minimizing 1+g(v), with smallest-id ties and
strictly decreasing g, until it reaches the goal.

> Watch a route disappear and return in a five-cell corridor

On a one-by-five free corridor, the backward labels are 4, 3, 2, 1, 0.
Block cell (0,2): the start component loses every connection to the goal,
so old finite labels in that component must be invalidated. Reopen the
cell: the retained goal-side labels allow finite costs to propagate
back toward the start. These are actual raise and lower operations,
not a reconstructed animation or a new cold search.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dstar_corridor.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::algorithms::DStarLiteGrid p(dm::algorithms::grid_obstacle_mask(1,5),{0,0},{0,4});
  const auto first=p.repair_trace();
  p.update_cells({{{0,2},true}});const auto blocked=p.repair_trace();
  p.update_cells({{{0,2},false}});const auto reopened=p.repair_trace();
  const auto text=[](std::uint64_t x){return x==dm::algorithms::d_star_infinity?std::string("inf"):std::to_string(x);};
  std::print("| stage | found | cost when found | pops | lowerings | raisings | final g labels |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  const std::array<const char*,3> names{"initial","blocked middle","reopened"};
  const std::array<const dm::algorithms::DStarTrace*,3> traces{&first,&blocked,&reopened};
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<traces.size();++i) {
    const auto& t=*traces[i];std::string labels;
    for(auto value:t.final_g) {if(!labels.empty())labels+=" ";labels+=text(value);}
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",names[i],t.repair.route.found,t.repair.route.found?std::format("{:.0f}",t.repair.route.cost):"—",t.repair.work.pops,t.repair.work.lowerings,t.repair.work.raisings,labels);
  }
  std::print("\n| blocked-stage cell | action | g before | rhs before | g after | rhs after |\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  const std::array<const char*,3> action{"refresh key","lower","raise"};
  for(const auto& s:blocked.steps)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",s.vertex,action[static_cast<int>(s.action)],text(s.g_before),text(s.rhs_before),text(s.g_after),text(s.rhs_after));
  auto a=dm::algorithms::plot_grid_route(blocked.blocked,blocked.repair.route,blocked.start,blocked.goal);
  auto b=dm::algorithms::plot_grid_route(reopened.blocked,reopened.repair.route,reopened.start,reopened.goal);
  a.title("Blocked middle: no route");b.title("Reopened: cost four restored");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(a).add(b).size(1100,450)
   .title("Retained labels handle both cost increases and decreases").fit_panels()
   .save_svg("build/dstar_corridor.svg");
}
