\caption(Across six grid widths, unchanged/moved/off-route queries perform zero pops. A route obstacle requires real repair; the plotted comparison uses a fresh run of the same kernel on the final map.)
\alttext(Two panels compare initial, retained-repair and fresh-final queue pops over powers-of-two widths and show the edit-to-fresh ratio alongside zero-pop warm queries.)

In this family each route edit takes exactly two more pops than its
fresh comparison: 94 versus 92 at width 32, and 3,070 versus 3,068
at width 1,024. Those curves nearly overlap in the left panel; their
ratio approaches one from above in the right panel. The zero-pop
unchanged, moved and off-route queries are the observed savings here.

> Actual key refresh, resumable capture and safe ownership

The trace captures actual pre/post pop operands, initial/final g/rhs,
current occupancy and initial queued keys. It is not a replay. Its
logical budget is 5N+Q+P entries: one grid cell and four label elements
per vertex, Q initial queue records and P captured pop records. The
complete base cost is checked before copying; each extra pop entry is
checked before mutating the queue or labels. Scalar metadata, working
buffers, paths and allocator bytes are not part of that logical count.
The default cap is one million; zero disables only this optional cap.

If a cap stops a repair after some completed pops, the persistent state
remains usable. A later repair resumes; work_before identifies pending
interval work, while the new steps vector contains only the resumed
call's pops. Work resets only after a successful result is prepared.
For the original four-cell input, base cost 21 plus four pops gives 25
entries. A cap of 24 stops before the last pop and can then be resumed.

All shape/cell/endpoint limits are checked before construction. Invalid
edit coordinates are checked across the entire batch before any edit;
invalid moves leave the start unchanged. The class preallocates its
indexed queue's full-cell capacity. Copies retain independent buffers
and that capacity; assignment constructs a complete replacement before
swapping, preserving the old planner on allocation failure.

The next moving-start case keeps a nonempty frontier. Its first stale
stored key is (8,1), while the current key is (10,1). The vertex is
requeued without changing its g or rhs. This is the lazy-key repair
that a stateless shortest-path call cannot demonstrate.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dstar_boundaries.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto grid=dm::algorithms::grid_obstacle_mask(2,2);
  dm::algorithms::DStarLiteGrid exact(grid,{0,0},{1,1});
  const auto full=exact.repair_trace(25);
  dm::algorithms::DStarLiteGrid limited(grid,{0,0},{1,1});int caps=0;
  try{(void)limited.repair_trace(24);}catch(const std::length_error&){++caps;}
  const auto resumed=limited.repair_trace();
  std::print("Exact entries {}; cap rejections {}; resumed-call pops {}; earlier pending pops {}; total interval pops {}; cost {:.0f}.\n",full.captured_entries,caps,resumed.steps.size(),resumed.work_before.pops,resumed.repair.work.pops,resumed.repair.route.cost);
  const auto saved=exact.grid();int invalid=0;
  try{exact.update_cells({{{0,0},true},{{9,9},false}});}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++invalid;}
  try{exact.move_start({-1,0});}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++invalid;}
  std::print("Invalid batch/move rejections {}/2; occupancy unchanged {}; start unchanged {}.\n",invalid,exact.grid()==saved,exact.start()==std::pair<int,int>{0,0});
  auto copy=exact;copy.update_cells({{{0,0},true}});
  std::print("Independent copy: original found {}; changed copy found {}.\n",exact.repair().route.found,copy.repair().route.found);
  dm::algorithms::DStarLiteGrid moved(dm::algorithms::grid_obstacle_mask(5,7),{2,0},{2,6});
  (void)moved.repair();moved.move_start({0,0});const auto t=moved.repair_trace();
  const auto it=std::find_if(t.steps.begin(),t.steps.end(),[](const auto& s){return s.action==dm::algorithms::DStarAction::rekey;});
  if(it==t.steps.end() || it->g_before!=it->g_after || it->rhs_before!=it->rhs_after)throw std::runtime_error("missing actual key refresh");
  std::print("Moved-start query: cost {:.0f}; total pops {}; key refreshes {}; first refreshed vertex {}; key ({},{}) -> ({},{}).\n",t.repair.route.cost,t.repair.work.pops,t.repair.work.key_refreshes,it->vertex,it->old_key.first,it->old_key.second,it->current_key.first,it->current_key.second);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(80,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=3.8;box.height=1.2;box.font_size=24;
  d.node("old",{0,1.5},std::format("stored key ({},{})\nvertex {}",it->old_key.first,it->old_key.second,it->vertex),box)
   .node("new",{5.6,1.5},std::format("current key ({},{})\nmodifier = {}",it->current_key.first,it->current_key.second,t.key_modifier),box)
   .node("result",{2.8,-1},"reinsert with current key\ng and rhs unchanged",box)
   .edge("old","new","recalculate").edge("new","result")
   .text({2.8,2.9},"An actual stale moving-start key is refreshed",26)
   .save_svg("build/dstar_boundaries.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Exact entries 25; cap rejections 1; resumed-call pops 1; earlier pending pops 3; total interval pops 4; cost 2.
Invalid batch/move rejections 2/2; occupancy unchanged true; start unchanged true.
Independent copy: original found true; changed copy found false.
Moved-start query: cost 8; total pops 28; key refreshes 14; first refreshed vertex 13; key (8,1) -> (10,1).
\image(figures/dstar_boundaries.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The complete four-cell trace uses 25 logical entries. A one-short cap leaves resumable progress; invalid batch/move controls and independent copies preserve owned state. A separate moving query captures a real (8,1) to (10,1) key refresh.)
\alttext(A native diagram contrasts the actual stored and current priority of vertex 13 after moving the start, then requeues it while leaving both labels unchanged.)

> Verification and limits

Native tests compare all 41,472 three-by-three obstacle-mask/terminal
combinations against independent breadth-first distances. A further
29,952 cold/flip/restore repairs cover every two-by-three mask and
endpoint pair. Their trace certificates replay the actual indexed-open
entries, integer keys, Bellman backups and lower/raise/refresh branches,
then compare all final labels, queue membership, termination and work
counters. Seeded batch edits and moving starts add 2,400 changing-map
queries, including all three pop actions.

Boundary tests cover blocked start/goal, empty/ragged grids, complete
input validation, exact/one-short/disabled caps, resumed work accounting,
copy/assignment independence, malformed supplied plot paths and a
50,001-cell corridor with block/reopen and warm reuse. The native plot
helper validates route feasibility and cost but is not an independent
optimality certificate; the breadth-first oracle supplies that check.

The planner solves a known occupancy snapshot with a fixed goal. It
does not infer obstacles, sense a map, choose when to move, model
uncertainty or handle weighted/diagonal travel. Its incremental state
can save repeated work, while a large change can still require broad
repair. Preserving those qualifications is essential when using the
original D*-style interface to discuss a genuine incremental method.

> References

S. Koenig and M. Likhachev (2002), [D* Lite](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~motionplanning/papers/sbp_papers/integrated3/koenig_dstarlite_aaai02b.pdf),
AAAI, 476–483, develops the g/rhs priority repair and moving-start modifier.
This implementation uses its basic consistency termination, an indexed
heap, deterministic row-major ties, exact bounded unit-distance labels
and explicit blocked-terminal conventions. A. Stentz (1994),
[Optimal and Efficient Path Planning for Partially-Known Environments](https://publications.ri.cmu.edu/optimal-and-efficient-path-planning-for-partially-known-environments),
ICRA 4, 3310–3317, introduces the earlier D* method. The persistent API
here is D* Lite; the original one-shot route remains a convenience call
that begins with fresh planner state.

